# Seed Data and Reset Guide — {{project_slug}}

The app must not look empty in sandbox review. Every implemented feature must have representative seed data and test credentials.

## Required Seeder Policy

- Each user-facing feature slice must add or update Laravel seeders.
- Create multiple focused seeders, not one opaque dump: test users/roles, org/tenant structure, feature records, edge states, and relationship/linkage records.
- Seed at least 10 realistic records for each feature unless the feature naturally has fewer valid states.
- Seed all roles/personas needed to test the planned flows through normal authentication.
- Seed edge cases where useful: draft, active, approved, rejected, overdue, archived, failed, or escalated records.
- Seed enough users to test the whole app flow through the normal login screen.
- Keep test credentials, roles, permissions, recommended flows, and reset commands documented here after every slice.

## Test Credentials

Update this table as users/roles are created.

| Role | Email / Username | Password | Permissions Scope | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Admin | admin@example.com | Generated by seeder | All records | Default administrative review account |

Seed credentials must cover every role, persona, department, tenant, approval state, ownership path, and restricted/edge-case user needed to test the whole app flow.

Reviewers must sign in through the normal login page with the seeded email/username and password. Do not add a navbar impersonation/user-switching UI unless the product requirements explicitly ask for one.

Also document each seeded user's role, name, email/username, password, permissions scope, seeded records they own or can access, and what flow that identity can test.

## Reset Sandbox Data

Use this when you want a clean sandbox database with seeded records:

```bash
php artisan migrate:fresh --seed
```

If the app runs through Docker, use the project-specific equivalent:

```bash
docker compose exec app php artisan migrate:fresh --seed
```

## Remove Test Seed Data

Test seed data is for sandbox and review. For production:

1. Do not run test/demo seeders in production.
2. Keep test seeders guarded by environment checks or explicit seeder class names.
3. Remove or disable sample credentials before production deployment.
4. Confirm production deployment uses real admin provisioning from `docs/user-manual/production-deploy.md`.

